					</outline>
					<outline text="&lt;img src=&quot;screenshots/example2.png&quot;&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Example 3">
					<outline text="A post with two &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2024/03/22/025201.html?title=vermont&quot;&gt;inline images&lt;/a&gt; about driving to Vermont. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="&lt;img src=&quot;screenshots/example3.png&quot;&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Example 4">
					<outline text="A titled post with a list of four &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2024/03/15/015719.html&quot;&gt;numbered items&lt;/a&gt;. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="&lt;img src=&quot;screenshots/example4.png&quot;&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Example 5">
					<outline text="A post with a &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2024/01/21.html#a194147&quot;&gt;podcast enclosure&lt;/a&gt;. Amazing that this makes it out to the front matter, but that's the power of simple standards. We just did what made sense, probably wouldn't have done it if it wasn't really easy. :-)">
					</outline>
					<outline text="&lt;img src=&quot;screenshots/example5.png&quot;&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Example 6">
					<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2024/03/01/203935.html&quot;&gt;First post&lt;/a&gt; of the month, note that it wraps back to the previous month in the urlPrev value.  ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="&lt;img src=&quot;screenshots/example6.png&quot;&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Example 7">
					<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2024/03/12/120111.html&quot;&gt;A bulleted list&lt;/a&gt; and a value for description. Might be useful when displaying the story in a reader app. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="&lt;img src=&quot;screenshots/example8.png&quot;&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Example 8">
					<outline text="This was the &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/22.html#a160031&quot;&gt;most recent post&lt;/a&gt; on scripting.com when the screen shot was taken. Note that there is a urlPrev value in the YAML text, but no urlNext. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="&lt;img src=&quot;screenshots/example9.png&quot;&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Notes">
					<outline text="To start this project I took the OPML source for Scripting News, publicly archived &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/Scripting-News/tree/master/blog/opml&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;, and had Claude write a utility app that converted it to markdown. We iterated over that app, and at the same time built a Markdown Browser, to guide us in getting the parts to fit together. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We took screen shots of the Markdown for several example posts, hoping to illustrate a range of features. That's what you see on the page above.  ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="No documentation yet. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Because I was doing all this work with Claude we were able to ask about prior art for all the stuff we're doing. We were guided in the choice of values for the front matter by two sources: &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/oldSchoolBlog&quot;&gt;Old School&lt;/a&gt; which is the OPML-to-HTML rendering app I've been using for my blog since May 2017, and &lt;a href=&quot;https://cyber.harvard.edu/rss/rss.html&quot;&gt;RSS 2.0&lt;/a&gt;, which has names for most of the metadata you might use for a post. Not a surprise, same thing happened when I was designing the &lt;a href=&quot;https://xmlrpc.com/storyreader/story2198.md&quot;&gt;MetaWeblog API&lt;/a&gt;, it turned out we were just transporting RSS 2.0 items. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We abide by the &lt;a href=&quot;https://this.how/standards/&quot;&gt;Rules for standards-makers&lt;/a&gt;. We want to interop with everyone we can. Examples include: Jekyll, Hugo, Eleventy, Astro and Obsidian.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The &lt;i&gt;source&lt;/i&gt; attribute (or front matter field) provides the address of the feed it came from. When displaying a post you might want to refer to some info from the feed, such as its title, link, icon.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The full &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/raw/refs/heads/main/examples/scriptingInMarkdown/archive.zip&quot;&gt;archive&lt;/a&gt; of scripting.com between May 2017 and October 2026 is available as a download, in Markdown, these are the same files we're using on our test server. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/blob/main/readme.md&quot;&gt;readme.md&lt;/a&gt; on this site has some philosophy you might want to read, and if you have questions or comments, use the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/issues/1&quot;&gt;Issues&lt;/a&gt; section. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Finally, as a teaser, this is what &lt;a href=&quot;screenshots/rendered0.png&quot;&gt;Example 0 looks like&lt;/a&gt; in our Markdown browser. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="readme.md">
				<outline text="# The Markdown web">
					<outline text="Getting started with a simpler, safer and more efficient web for writers and readers. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Writers and readers">
					<outline text="The goal is to create a simple web that works better for both writers and readers. ">
						<outline text="* Writers have full control over the written word. They can link to other Markdown pages, locally or on other sites. They can use whatever editor they like for all their writing. ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* Readers have full control over appearance -- e.g. font, size, spacing, colors -- everything having to do with how text is displayed, so there is a consistency with stories written by all authors and that is totally determined by the reader. The software they use may hardcode the style, or offer whatever user-editable options they want. ">
						</outline>
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Markdown and YAML front matter">
					<outline text="We define YAML frontmatter that activates features in the browser we've made. If your files have the same concepts as ours, please use the ones we've defined for maximum interop.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We strip any HTML from the files. And front matter we don't understand is ignored. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The YAML vocabulary we use comes from RSS 2.0, including title, author, pubDate, description, generator, source and enclosure. I've added others from the CMS we use or were invented for this application including urlPrev, urlNext, image, inlineImage, metaImage. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We've found that there's a very good match between the metadata for feed items and written documents. Not exactly surprising, we keep running into this consistency everywhere we work in the written web. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Examples">
					<outline text="To get the process rolling, I've prepared nine &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/blob/main/examples/readme.md&quot;&gt;example Markdown web docs&lt;/a&gt; for you to consider. No docs yet.  ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="They are all the Markdown versions of posts that appeared on Scripting News sometime between May 2017 and October 2026.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Historic context">
					<outline text="JavaScript was introduced in 1995.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="CSS was introduced to the web in 1996. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The original web as designed by Tim Berners-Lee was just for documents, and the same is true for the Web of Markdown.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Just for writing and reading. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Community">
					<outline text="I first wrote about this on October 6 in &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/06/005559.html?title=webOfMarkdown&quot;&gt;The Web of Markdown&lt;/a&gt;. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="The next day &lt;a href=&quot;https://andrewshell.org/2026/10/the-web-of-markdown/&quot;&gt;Andrew Shell&lt;/a&gt; got a browser working, and all of a sudden it feels like someone else got the excitement.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="A few days later, Dirk Primbs &lt;a href=&quot;https://unprompted.dirkprimbs.de/posts/engine-update-markdown.html&quot;&gt;wrote&lt;/a&gt; about using &lt;a href=&quot;https://unprompted.dirkprimbs.de/llms.txt&quot;&gt;llms.txt&lt;/a&gt; to provide a list of Markdown files in a site, with links and short descriptions.  ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="I had already been playing around with ideas for a non-HTML web browser, I got excited about this idea and wrote the original post, but when I saw the excellent ideas that Andrew and Dirk had, I rolled up my sleeves and decided to get it working with the archived content of my blog between 2017 and 2026, a period when the format was standardized, and very little breakage (I did try out a few ideas that didn't go very far, I wouldn't carry those forward).">
					</outline>
					<outline text="What I hope to do by starting this repo and sharing my progress is to get something that moves quickly. Interop is what it's about. And getting back to the original idea of the web as a place for writing and reading. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Where we're at">
					<outline text="We've been working on software that implements this but wanted to put the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/blob/main/examples/readme.md&quot;&gt;examples&lt;/a&gt; out first to see what people think.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="It would be especially interesting to use this framework for a &quot;social web&quot; application like Twitter, Mastodon or Bluesky. Then we would have agreement over what text is, and the products could interop. In the indieweb world they call this POSSE. It would realize that goal. And allow us to easily write cross-application products.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Markdown defines a very good format for text on the web without all the dangerous stuff, and the front matter metadata we define can take care of the rest. Imagine a social web where you can use whatever writing tool you like and for reading you can tweak the display for maximum comfort and ease of use. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We've made it work for blogging, but the same tech could be used for slides, code, whatever you can think of. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="And all the while writers can still use whatever tools they want, and readers get text that is readable in ways they like to read.  ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="CSS and JavaScript are only supported on the client side. Writers have no say in how it looks beyond simple styling -- bold, italic. And they can't create apps with logic, as would be implemented in JavaScript or another programming language. This is a medium for reading and writing only. But you are free to add scripting to your client. Some people would like to have the ability to build new products out of the basic capabilities of the web. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Any Markdown file will work. But it does not do anything with HTML files. This is the web of Markdown, not HTML. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Notes">
					<outline text="For more context see &lt;a href=&quot;https://textcasting.org/&quot;&gt;textcasting.org&lt;/a&gt;.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Obviously we're going to make this work extremely well with RSS 2.0 and OPML. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="I also love WebSockets, so expect some of that as well. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="">
			</outline>
			<outline text="">
			</outline>
			<outline text="examples.md" isComment="true">
				<outline text="# Examples of Markdown text with YAML front matter">
					<outline text="How the pieces fit together in our web of Markdown files.  ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Writers and readers">
					<outline text="The goal is to create a simple web that works for both writers and readers. ">
						<outline text="* Writers have full control over the written word, and can link the story with other pages on other sites, and give it an optional title, and use whatever editor they like for all their writing. After using the web for over 30 years, this is what I want, what I insist on. ">
						</outline>
						<outline text="* Readers have full control over font, size, spacing, colors -- everything having to do with how text is displayed, so there is a consistency with stories written by both of us. The software they use may hard-code the style, or offer whatever user-editable options they want. ">
						</outline>
					</outline>
					<outline text="We've been working on software that implements this, it's not ready to be deployed yet, but I wanted to share the basic principles of the project, provide examples that work, and field comments and questions. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We've made it work for blogging, but the same tech could be used for slides, code, whatever you can think of. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="And all the while writers can still use whatever tools they want, and readers get text that is readable in ways they like to read.  ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Markdown and YAML front matter">
					<outline text="Any Markdown file will work. It's the HTML of the Markdown web. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We define YAML frontmatter that activates features in the browser we've made. If your files have the same concepts as ours, please use the ones we've defined for maximum interop.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We strip any HTML from the files. And front matter we don't understand is ignored. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We use the nomenclature of RSS 2.0 for our front matter, but we plan to read the values put there by other popular software. We want the maximum interop possible. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Following are examples. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Example 0">
					<outline text="sdfasdfasdf">
					</outline>
					<outline text="&lt;%exampleMarkdownCode (@workspace.markdownweb.example0)%&gt;">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Example 0 " isComment="true">
					<outline text="```">
					</outline>
					<outline text="---">
					</outline>
					<outline text="author: &quot;Dave Winer&quot;">
					</outline>
					<outline text="pubDate: &quot;Sun, 31 Mar 2024 16:42:41 GMT&quot;">
					</outline>
					<outline text="generator: &quot;opmlToMarkdown v0.5.9&quot;">
					</outline>
					<outline text="urlPrev: &quot;181216.md&quot;">
					</outline>
					<outline text="urlNext: &quot;a161730.md&quot;">
					</outline>
					<outline text="---">
					</outline>
					<outline text="```">
					</outline>
					<outline text="I start a fresh outline for this blog on the first of every month, so tomorrow I will [archive](https://github.com/scripting/Scripting-News/tree/master/blog/opml) my blog.opml file on GitHub, start a new month, and delete the previous month. I've been doing that since 2017, when I finally forever gave up trying to interop with the great blogging silos (ie Twitter and Facebook mainly). I think outlining is like double-diamond skiing, but for writing.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="tmp.md" isComment="true">
				<outline text="### Example 0">
				</outline>
				<outline text="The file is at https://dave.writersweb.org/2024/03/31/a164241.md. This is its text, exactly as it is on the server.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="```">
				</outline>
				<outline text="---">
				</outline>
				<outline text="author: &quot;Dave Winer&quot;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="pubDate: &quot;Sun, 31 Mar 2024 16:42:41 GMT&quot;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="generator: &quot;opmlToMarkdown v0.5.9&quot;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="urlPrev: &quot;181216.md&quot;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="urlNext: &quot;a161730.md&quot;">
				</outline>
				<outline text="---">
				</outline>
				<outline text="I start a fresh outline for this blog on the first of every month, so tomorrow I will [archive](https://github.com/scripting/Scripting-News/tree/master/blog/opml) my blog.opml file on GitHub, start a new month, and delete the previous month. I've been doing that since 2017, when I finally forever gave up trying to interop with the great blogging silos (ie Twitter and Facebook mainly). I think outlining is like double-diamond skiing, but for writing.">
				</outline>
				<outline text="```">
				</outline>
			</outline>
		</outline>
		<outline text="bundle //build script" isComment="true">
			<outline text="nodeEditorSuite.utilities.buildMarkdownWebRepo ()">
			</outline>
		</outline>
